.TH RETROSAVER 1 "2026-09-01" "retrosaver 0.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
retrosaver \- retro screensavers for GNOME on Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B retrosaver
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B retrosaver
brings back late\-1990s XScreenSaver display modules \(em fractals, the
.B atlantis
fish tank, flying toasters, spinning pipes \(em on GNOME desktops running Wayland, where
the traditional screensaver daemon no longer functions.
.PP
It does not fork, patch, or vendor XScreenSaver. It reuses the display modules that Debian
and Ubuntu already package, and supplies only the piece GNOME dropped: an idle trigger and
a fullscreen wrapper. All the artwork belongs to XScreenSaver.
.PP
Idle is detected through
.BR org.gnome.Mutter.IdleMonitor ,
the only idle signal GNOME exposes, since its compositor implements no Wayland idle
protocol.
.SS Stages
Three stages, all timed from when the session goes idle. Any user activity at any stage
tears everything down and re\-arms from zero.
.TP
.B Saver
5 minutes idle by default. Launch a random display module fullscreen, always\-on\-top,
with the pointer hidden.
.TP
.B Lock
20 minutes idle by default. Kill the module and hand off to GNOME's own lock screen via
.BR "loginctl lock\-session" .
.TP
.B Blank
22 minutes idle by default. Power the display off, by handing
.B idle\-delay
back to gnome\-shell rather than poking DPMS behind the compositor's back.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Runtime
.TP
.B daemon
Run the idle state machine. Normally started by the systemd user unit, not by hand.
Re-reads its configuration on
.B SIGHUP
and whenever the config file changes. See
.B SIGNALS
below.
.TP
.BI run " [module]"
Launch one module fullscreen. Picks at random when
.I module
is omitted. Refuses to stack a second module over a running one.
.TP
.B stop
Tear down a running module and clear the runtime state. Safe at any time, and a clean
no\-op when nothing is running.
.TP
.B list
Print the discovered, selectable modules, one per line.
.SS Installation
.TP
.B setup
Run the preflight checks, write a config if none exists, enable the systemd user unit, and
take ownership of
.BR idle\-delay ,
saving the original value first.
.TP
.B teardown
Reverse
.BR setup :
disable the unit and restore the saved
.BR idle\-delay .
.SS Other
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B retrosaver
reads
.IR ~/.config/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf .
The file is shell\-shaped
.IR KEY=value ,
but it is
.B parsed, never sourced
\(em it lives in
.I $HOME
and is never executed. A commented example is installed at
.IR /usr/share/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf.example .
.TP
.B SAVER_DELAY
Seconds of idle before the screensaver starts. Default 300.
.TP
.B LOCK_AFTER
Seconds after the screensaver starts before the session locks. Default 900.
.B 0
disables locking.
.TP
.B BLANK_AFTER
Seconds after locking before the display powers off. Default 120.
.B 0
disables blanking, as does
.B LOCK_AFTER=0
\(em blanking without locking is meaningless.
.TP
.B EXCLUDE
Space\-separated module names never to pick. The default excludes modules that need image
assets, network access, or elevated capabilities and misbehave standalone.
.TP
.B INCLUDE
Optional. If non\-empty, pick only from this space\-separated list.
.PP
Changes take effect as soon as the file is saved: the daemon watches it with inotify and
re-arms itself.
.B "systemctl \-\-user reload retrosaver"
does the same on demand. A reload behaves like user activity \(em any module on screen is
torn down and the stages re-arm from zero. A configuration that fails to parse is
.B not
applied: the error is logged and the daemon keeps the settings it already had, because
exiting would leave the session with no screensaver and no auto\-lock.
.PP
Modules are discovered by intersecting the basenames of
.I /usr/share/xscreensaver/config/*.xml
with the executables in
.IR /usr/libexec/xscreensaver/ ,
so helper binaries are never selected and the list follows the installed packages.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf
User configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/retrosaver/idle\-delay.orig
The
.B idle\-delay
value saved at
.BR setup ,
restored by
.BR teardown .
.TP
.I /usr/share/retrosaver/retrosaver.conf.example
Commented example configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/retrosaver.service
The systemd user unit. Shipped by the package but deliberately not enabled;
.B retrosaver setup
enables it, because enabling a
.B systemd \-\-user
unit needs the user's own session bus.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/retrosaver.pid
PID of the running module. Every PID read back is checked against
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
before being signalled, because the file can be stale after a crash and PIDs are recycled.
.SH CAVEATS
.SS This is not a screen locker
A display module is an ordinary X11 window and cannot secure the session; X11 grabs do not
work under XWayland. Real security comes from the lock stage handing off to GNOME's own
lock screen. Anyone wanting the screensaver itself to lock is out of luck on Wayland, by
design.
.SS The daemon owns the idle policy
.B setup
sets
.B org.gnome.desktop.session idle\-delay
to
.BR 0 .
That is what stops gnome\-shell blanking the screen out from under the screensaver, and it
also disables GNOME's idle\-dim.
.B "Consequently, if the daemon is not running there is no auto\-lock at all."
The unit uses
.B Restart=always
and
.B ExecStopPost
to restore
.B idle\-delay
on any exit, and
.B teardown
restores it permanently.
.SS Other limitations
Modules that grab and distort a desktop screenshot render a solid colour, because
.B grim
does not work under GNOME's compositor. GL modules run through XWayland and keep the GPU
awake. On a laptop,
.B sleep\-inactive\-battery\-timeout
may suspend the machine before the lock and blank stages land.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration file and re-arm, without restarting. The process, its D-Bus
connection and its ownership of
.B idle\-delay
are all preserved, where a restart drops them for a moment. The systemd unit exposes this
as
.BR "systemctl \-\-user reload retrosaver" .
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Tear down cleanly, stopping any running module and restoring
.BR idle\-delay .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every state transition logs to standard error, so
.B journalctl \-\-user \-u retrosaver
reads as a clear trace.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xscreensaver (1),
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR gsettings (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5)
.PP
Project home: https://github.com/c-premus/retrosaver
.SH AUTHOR
Chris <chris@example.com>.
.PP
The display modules themselves are XScreenSaver, by Jamie Zawinski and contributors.
.B retrosaver
is only the idle trigger and the fullscreen wrapper around them.
